01:01:25.890 --> 01:01:30.230
This was the question that launched everything was, who made the first bottle of water?

1413
01:01:30.350 --> 01:01:30.450
Right, right.

1414
01:01:30.650 --> 01:01:34.790
It was Jackson Spa, and the people who drank the water thought that it held therapeutic

1415
01:01:34.790 --> 01:01:38.970
properties because it came from like one of those mineral springs, a natural spring.

1416
01:01:39.430 --> 01:01:44.230
One of the biggies, Arrowhead, was established in 1917.

1417
01:01:44.590 --> 01:01:45.730
That's so early.

1418
01:01:45.730 --> 01:01:46.590
I know.

1419
01:01:47.290 --> 01:01:51.890
Arrowhead water is sourced from the mountain springs in California, Colorado, and Canada.

1420
01:01:52.150 --> 01:01:54.270
It's bottled in California, even today.

1421
01:01:54.670 --> 01:01:56.270
Then we get up to Evian.

1422
01:01:56.870 --> 01:02:02.690
That was not established until 1978, almost 70 years after Arrowhead.

1423
01:02:02.930 --> 01:02:05.910
Their water comes from a spring in the French Alps.

1424
01:02:06.270 --> 01:02:10.050
Fiji water, as held by David Wallace, comes from Fiji.

1425
01:02:10.050 --> 01:02:16.150
But the big shift in bottled water came when Pepsi and Coca-Cola got into the game by selling

1426
01:02:16.150 --> 01:02:17.670
purified water.

1427
01:02:18.730 --> 01:02:25.310
Purified water is basically municipal tap water, which is then filtered using a process

1428
01:02:25.310 --> 01:02:26.990
called reverse osmosis.

1429
01:02:27.350 --> 01:02:32.950
Spring water is also filtered, but the reverse osmosis is what purifies it, I guess.

1430
01:02:33.110 --> 01:02:34.290
That's the purified part.

1431
